//? Title: Quarter-turn convolutions and Euclidean graph equivariance
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Verify two transformation laws on the upstream fixtures. Rotate a
scalar image and check the corresponding rotation and channel shift.
Rotate, reflect, translate and permute a point cloud and check the
transformed positions and scalar features.
)

> Tie kernels using relative orientation

The four rotations of a square form the group C4. The lifting
convolution makes four orientation channels from one scalar image by
rotating a single spatial kernel. A second convolution ties its kernels
using the difference between input and output orientations. A quarter
turn of the input therefore rotates the spatial output and shifts the
orientation channels cyclically. The intermediate pointwise ReLU
preserves this correspondence.

> Combine invariant scalars with relative coordinates

Squared distances and scalar node features are unchanged by rigid
motions. The graph layer uses these quantities to compute scalar
messages. Multiplying a message by the relative position makes a
coordinate update that rotates or reflects with the input. Translation
cancels from relative positions and is restored by the residual
connection. Permuting the nodes simultaneously permutes the features
and both axes of the edge mask, preserving the same sum or mean.

> Check both transformation laws

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/equivariant_networks.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
constexpr auto precision = nn::DType::Float64;

int main() {
    nn::set_num_threads(2);
    constexpr int side = 9, nodes = 6;
    std::mt19937 image_rng(18389), graph_rng(18390);
    std::uniform_real_distribution<double> image_draw(-.7, .7), graph_draw(-1., 1.);
    const auto draw = [](const nn::Shape& shape, auto& rng, auto& distribution) {
        std::vector<double> values(nn::shape_numel(shape));
        for (auto& v : values) v = distribution(rng);
        return nn::from_vector(values, shape);
    };
    const auto image = draw({1, side, side, 1}, image_rng, image_draw);
    const auto lift = nn::c4_lift_kernel(draw({3, 3}, image_rng, image_draw));
    const auto kernel = nn::c4_group_kernel(draw({3, 3, 4}, image_rng, image_draw));
    const auto rotate = [](const auto& tensor) { return nn::reverse(nn::transpose(tensor, {0, 2, 1, 3}), {1}); };
    const auto run_image = [&](const auto& tensor) {
        return nn::conv2d(nn::relu(nn::conv2d(tensor, lift)), kernel);
    };
    const auto turned = rotate(image);
    const auto base = run_image(image), rotated = run_image(turned);
    const auto expected = rotate(nn::concat({
        nn::slice_axis(base, 3, 3, 4), nn::slice_axis(base, 3, 0, 3)}, 3));
    const double rotation_error = nn::max(nn::abs(expected - rotated)).item();

    const auto positions = draw({nodes, 3}, graph_rng, graph_draw);
    const auto features = draw({nodes, 1}, graph_rng, graph_draw);
    const auto edges = nn::expand_dims(1. - nn::eye(nodes, precision), -1);
    const std::vector<std::int64_t> permutation{3, 5, 1, 0, 4, 2};
    const auto matrix = nn::tensor({0., 1., 0., -1., 0., 0., 0., 0., -1.}, {3, 3}, precision);
    const auto translation = nn::tensor({.7, -.5, 1.2}, {1, 3}, precision);
    const auto transform = [&](const auto& coordinates) {
        return nn::matmul(nn::take_rows(coordinates, permutation), matrix) + translation;
    };
    const auto changed_positions = transform(positions);
    const auto changed_features = nn::take_rows(features, permutation);
    const auto standard = nn::euclidean_graph_step(positions, features, edges);
    const auto transformed = nn::euclidean_graph_step(changed_positions, changed_features, edges);
    const double coordinate_error = nn::max(nn::abs(transform(standard.positions) - transformed.positions)).item();
    const double scalar_error = nn::max(nn::abs(nn::take_rows(standard.features, permutation) - transformed.features)).item();
    if (std::max({rotation_error, coordinate_error, scalar_error}) > 1e-12)
        throw std::runtime_error("Equivariance failed");
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto panels = nn::reshape(nn::transpose(nn::concat({base, rotated}, 0), {0, 3, 1, 2}),
                                   {8, side, side, 1});
    nn::save_image_grid(panels, "build/equivariant_networks.png", 4,
                        nn::min(panels).item(), nn::max(panels).item(), 6, 0., 15);
    nn::save_npz("build/equivariant_c4.npz", {{"image", image}, {"rotated", turned}, {"lift", lift},
        {"kernel", kernel}, {"output", base}, {"rotated_output", rotated}});
    nn::save_npz("build/equivariant_graph.npz", {{"positions", positions}, {"features", features},
        {"updated_positions", standard.positions}, {"updated_features", standard.features},
        {"transformed_input", changed_positions}, {"transformed_features", changed_features},
        {"transformed_output", transformed.positions}, {"transformed_scalar_output", transformed.features}});
    std::print("C4 maximum error {:.2e}. Point graph coordinate error {:.2e}. Scalar error {:.2e}.\n",
               rotation_error, coordinate_error, scalar_error);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
C4 maximum error 4.44e-16. Point graph coordinate error 2.22e-16. Scalar error 0.00e+00.
\image(figures/equivariant_networks.png)
// result_end

The first row displays the four orientation channels of the original
image. The second row displays the channels after a quarter turn.
The common color scale makes the rotation and cyclic shift visible.
The numerical checks compare all the activations and both graph
outputs directly.

The convolution follows the weight-tying construction in
[Group Equivariant Convolutional Networks|https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.07576].
The graph step uses a fixed scalar message and the relative-coordinate update from
[E(n) Equivariant Graph Neural Networks|https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09844].
